From 18b583dec8c8aaec35f79b281cdf57abead7edfd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 17:24:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(marketing): route landing "Sign in" and "Live app" to
 this origin
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Reported: on vowos.bridgebox.ai the header "Sign in" and "Live app" controls
navigate to robertsenterprises.bridgebox.ai — a real production tenant — instead
of staying on the public marketing origin.

Root cause: the famous.ai bundle resolves its live-app origin as

    VITE_LIVE_APP_ORIGIN ?? "https://robertsenterprises.bridgebox.ai"

and reads that env var via `import.meta.env[key]` with a *dynamic* key, which
Vite cannot statically replace. At runtime the lookup is always undefined, so
the hardcoded tenant origin always wins. Its route table is also
`{home: "/", signIn: "/"}`, so even a corrected origin would land sign-in on the
marketing page rather than the app.

Compounding it: these controls are <button>s that navigate via JS, not <a href>.
The existing normalizer only rewrote anchors, and its click handler only acted on
`tagName === 'A'` for live-app intent — so neither control was ever reachable by
it. Only "Book a demo" (a text-matched button case) worked, which is why the
breakage looked partial.

Fix: the capture-phase click interceptor now handles buttons and [role=button]
as well as anchors, and maps visible control text to an explicit same-origin
destination: live app -> /demoapp, sign in -> /login, live demo -> /demo,
book a demo/request a plan/free trial -> /demo-request. Anchors pointing at the
tenant host or any *.vowos.bridgebox.ai host (all NXDOMAIN) are corrected first,
so a mislabelled link cannot escape the origin either.

Guard coverage: four new tests click the real controls on the real landing page
and assert where they land. Mutation-verified — with the previous marketing.html
the "live app" and "sign in" tests fail; with this one all 9 guard tests pass.

Note the guard's CTA tests poll the URL instead of using waitForURL(), which
waits for the `load` event by default and times out on the ~2.5 MB app bundle
even when navigation was correct, and they retry the click, because the landing
bundle hydrates after load and can replace the node mid-click.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01AAqkh5ZVeoEB5REorRbMyZ
